produced with exclusive support from  bearing a Ingelheim the biggest killer  in the Western world is thrombosis  thrombosis happens when a clot forms  inside an artery or a vein obstructing  the flow of blood when the clot blocks  one of the main arteries in the heart it  causes a heart attack if the clot forms  in a deep vein in the leg which can  happen when a patient is stuck in bed  it's called deep vein thrombosis  typically the leg swells up and the  danger is that the clot will dislodge  and travel to the lungs causing a  pulmonary embolism  sometimes a clot gets lodged in the  brain starving the tissue of oxygen and  causing a stroke for half a century  doctors have been using a drug called  warfarin to try to prevent thrombosis  warfarin is an anticoagulant it's  popularly known as a blood thinner but  in fact it doesn't affect the thickness  or viscosity of blood instead it  decreases the quantity of proteins that  normally allow blood to clot but  warfarin is a problematic drug and for  50 years scientists have been on a quest  to replace it the story actually begins  more than 50 years ago in the cattle  ranches of North America in the 1920s  farmers noticed their cows were bleeding  to death after minor medical procedures  and sometimes spontaneously this was  Depression era and to save money farmers  were feeding their cattle spoiled hay  made from sweet clover it turned out  that something in the sweet clover was  stopping the cows blood from clotting  properly in 1941 scientists identified  the culprit an anticoagulant called die  Coomer ol warfarin is a more potent form  of die Coomer ole and initially it was  used to poison rats but when a US army  recruit tried and failed to commit  suicide with warfarin doctors realized  they could turn this poison into a  life-saving drug  one early recipient was US President  Eisenhower who was given the drug after  suffering a heart attack in the Western  world today warfarin is a commonly  prescribed drug and you probably know  somebody who takes it as we age and  especially if our diet is full of  cholesterol fatty deposits buildup in  our veins and arteries these deposits  known as atherosclerosis plaques narrow  the vessels and make blood flow sluggish  sometimes a plaque ruptures exposing  material that activates platelets in the  blood and triggers a clot the platelets  clump together and are held in place by  a protein called fibrin fibrin is the  end product of the coagulation cascade  warfarin reduces fibrin production by  disrupting reactions further up the  Cascade it does this by inhibiting an  enzyme called vitamin K at POC side  reductase but warfarin has many  drawbacks it's slow to take action it  interacts with other drugs and people  vary in their response meaning patients  must be monitored regularly to get the  dose right because of these  complications say doctors some people at  risk from thrombosis are not treated in  the late-1980s  a growing awareness that thrombosis was  a major cause of heart attacks and  strokes fueled the search for a better  anticoagulant drug blood clotting is a  natural and often useful process it  stops us bleeding too much when we  injure ourselves so any new drug needs  to prevent dangerous clotting but  without causing excess bleeding bleeding  is one of the main side effects of  warfarin so what exactly were scientists  looking for the coagulation cascade is a  series of protein activation reactions  involving enzymes called proteases  designing inhibitors that target  proteases is tricky another key  challenge is to design a drug that can  be taken orally as warfarin can so that  patients can treat themselves at home  eventually scientists identified two  promising drug targets factor 10a and  thrombin  factor 10a plays a central role in the  coagulation cascade by catalyzing the  production of thrombin and  thrombomodulin  to fibrin the stuff which acts like the  glue in the clot studies showed that  both factor 10a and thrombin could be  suppressed without causing bleeding  complications  what's more solving the crystal  structure of thrombin helped scientists  find drugs to target this key enzyme in  the last decade clinical trials have put  factor 10a inhibitors and thrombin  inhibitors to the test after 50 years of  searching there are three new oral  anticoagulants on the market which could  eventually replace warfarin there's  dabigatran which inhibits thrombin and  then rivaroxaban and apixaban which  inhibit factor 10a other drugs are still  in clinical trials for doctors and  patients this is a real milestone having  a better treatment for thrombosis will  benefit many of us produced with  exclusive support from baring Ingelheim
 

